# Build cache

Two thirds of the firmware compile is code that does not change when the firmware changes: NimBLE
(189 of 354 translation units) and Berry (41). arduino-cli already caches their object files — what
destroys the cache is the wrapper's own build options, which change with every rig retune, and
arduino-cli cleaning the build path when they do. This plan gives both libraries prebuilt archives
that arduino-cli skips, and takes the rig numbers out of the compiler flags so that retuning a rig
stops costing a full rebuild.

## What is wrong now, and where

A full build is 354 translation units and about 3m45s on this machine: `build.options.json` is
written at 19:41:47 and the last object lands at 19:45:33. Of those units, 189 are NimBLE-Arduino
2.5.1 (188 `.cpp`/`.c` files under its `src`, one more from the sketch's include graph) and 41 are
`libraries/berry`; the ESP32 core contributes 54 and the first-party libraries the rest.

The objects are already cached. `scripts/arduino.py` keeps `build/compile` between runs precisely so
that arduino-cli can skip a file whose object and `.d` are newer than its sources, and its own
comment says so. What the cache does not survive is a change of *build options*: arduino-cli compares
`build.options.json` on every run and cleans the build path when any field differs —
`additionalFiles`, `compiler.optimization_flags`, `customBuildProperties`, `fqbn`,
`hardwareFolders`, `otherLibrariesFolders`, `sketchLocation`.

Rig numbers arrive through that path. `--define PUMP_FLOW_LPM=2.0f` becomes part of
`--build-property build.defines=…` (`scripts/arduino.py`, `build_defines`), so it lands in
`customBuildProperties` and wipes the build path; it also expands into `build.extra_flags` in the
ESP32 platform (`platform.txt:102`), which every compile recipe carries, so it is a flag change on
all 354 units as well. Nothing about `PUMP_FLOW_LPM` can affect NimBLE: NimBLE includes neither
`Machine.h` nor anything that includes it. The 189 NimBLE objects a retune discards are discarded for
nothing, and so are Berry's 41.

The two libraries also sit in different places. Berry is vendored at `libraries/berry` and its
`library.properties` is the repository's to change. NimBLE is installed by `--task libs` into
`Documents\Arduino\libraries\NimBLE-Arduino`, outside the tree, and is replaced there by
`arduino-cli lib install` or a version bump.

arduino-cli 1.4.1 has no cache of its own: `compile`'s flags include `--build-path`,
`--build-property`, `--clean`, `-j`, `--libraries`, `--library` and `--only-compilation-database`,
and nothing that reuses objects across build paths.

## The shape of the change

**A prebuilt archive per library.** The library specification's `precompiled=full` is the mechanism
for this: an archive in `src/{build.mcu}/` means the library's sources are not compiled and the
archive is linked instead; if no archive exists for the board, the sources compile as a fallback. The
fallback is what makes the design safe — a fresh clone, a new toolchain or a deleted archive is slow,
never broken. For this board `build.mcu` is `esp32s3` (`boards.txt:555`), so the archives are
`libraries/berry/src/esp32s3/libberry.a` and, in the staged copy below,
`src/esp32s3/libNimBLE.a`. Whether the platform also needs `ldflags=-l<name>` in `library.properties`
is one of the unknowns the spike settles.

**A staged copy of NimBLE the repository owns.** Because the archive has to sit inside a
`library.properties` the build answers to, `--task libs` stages the pinned version into
`firmware/gardener/build/third_party/NimBLE-Arduino/` (gitignored, regenerated from the pinned
version), and `compile_sketch()` passes a second `--libraries` for it — arduino-cli's `--libraries`
is repeatable. The installed copy in `Documents` stays as the source of the staging, not as the
library the sketch compiles against.

**Packing from objects that were just compiled.** After a build in which a precompiled library's
sources did compile, `build/compile/libraries/<Name>/` holds exactly the objects a correct archive
needs; `gcc-ar cr` over them is the pack, and it cannot drift from the flags because arduino-cli
produced them with those flags. Beside each archive, `archive.json` records the toolchain version,
the compile-flag string and a digest of every file under the library. The wrapper recomputes those
three before it invokes arduino-cli and **deletes** the archive when they differ, so a stale archive
is never linked; the build then takes the fallback path and re-packs at the end. That is also what
`--task archive` does explicitly, using `--only-compilation-database` to get the per-file commands
without compiling.

**Rig numbers in a generated header.** `--define` writes `firmware/gardener/rig_options.h`
(gitignored) and stops being passed to arduino-cli. `Machine.h` includes it behind
`#if __has_include("rig_options.h")` above its existing `#ifndef` defaults. The sketch folder is
already on the include path for every translation unit (`-I{build.source.path}` in the platform's
`compiler.cpreprocessor.flags`), so nothing about the flags changes; the defaults stay in
`Machine.h`, so a tree without the header, the host builds under `tools/` and a build made outside
the wrapper all behave exactly as today.

**One build path per configuration.** `COMPILE_DIR` gains a short hash of the FQBN, `build.defines`
and the core version, so switching a board or panel no longer destroys the other configuration's
97 MB of objects. `--task clean` removes all of them.

What is left after this is the ESP32 core's 54 units and the first-party libraries' ~120, which is
what an edit to the firmware genuinely touches.

## The alternatives, and why not

**ccache in front of the compiler.** The standard answer, and it needs no library-properties surgery:
a shim directory named as `compiler.path`, holding masquerade copies of `ccache.exe` for
`xtensa-esp32s3-elf-gcc`/`g++` and hardlinks for `gcc-ar`/`size`, silent when ccache is not
installed. It was set aside because it caches the *flags* as well: a rig retune still misses on every
unit, and NimBLE still recompiles — the rig-header change is needed either way, and with it the
archives remove the work instead of replaying it. It stays the fallback if `precompiled=full` turns
out not to work on this platform, and the two can be combined later without undoing either.

**A build path per rig, keeping the flags.** Cheaper than the header and it does stop one retune from
destroying another, but it fixes nothing for `--task clean`, a core upgrade or a fresh clone, and it
multiplies 97 MB per rig.

**Vendoring NimBLE into `libraries/`.** It gives the repository the `library.properties` and the
archive directory outright, at the price of 189 third-party files in the tree. Staging reaches the
same ownership without the tree carrying code the project does not own.

**Committing the archives.** A binary that has to be rebuilt on every core or toolchain change, in a
repository whose firmware target is a chip rather than a package. Both archives are gitignored and
regenerated; the fallback path makes their absence a cost, not a failure.

**ESP-IDF with CMake and Ninja.** A genuine build-system improvement — a shared prebuilt core,
first-class ccache, options in a CMake cache rather than an options hash that wipes a directory — and
a migration of the sketch, the libraries layout and the data-image task for a three-minute problem.

**Amalgamating Berry's sources.** Berry is vendored unmodified apart from `berry_conf.h` and
`be_port.c`; folding it into fewer translation units is a fork of the vendored library.

## The entry points it has to hold on

- `scripts/arduino.py`: `build`, `upload` (which shares `compile_sketch` with `--upload`), `data`,
  `libs`, `clean`, `monitor`. The packing has to hang off `compile_sketch`, not off `build`, or
  `--task upload` builds without it.
- `--task clean` keeps the archives, because they live beside the libraries rather than in `build/`;
  `--task libs` must invalidate and re-stage rather than keep a stale copy.
- The host builds — `tools/unit`, `tools/panel`, `tools/gfx/check.py` — read the same library sources
  without arduino-cli, so they must be unaffected by an archive appearing beside them.
- `build_opt.h` must stay untouched: it is hashed into `build.options.json` as `additionalFiles`, so
  putting rig numbers there would be the one way to keep the wipe and lose the win.

## How it is known to have worked

- **The spike, before any of this is written:** a two-file library with `precompiled=full` and an
  archive in `src/esp32s3/`, included from the sketch. `--verbose` shows no compile line for its
  source, a symbol from the archive appears in the `.elf`, and the board accepts the archive at all.
- A repeat build after the archives exist: `--verbose` carries no `NimBLE-Arduino` or `berry` compile
  lines and the `.elf` links. The same after `--task clean`.
- A rig retune, `--define PUMP_FLOW_LPM=2.0f`: `build.options.json` is unchanged, every NimBLE object
  keeps its mtime, and only the `Machine.h` includers and their dependents recompile.
- Deleting an `archive.json`, or editing a Berry source: the next build drops that archive, compiles
  the sources and re-packs — the staleness path, observed rather than assumed.
- On the device: `--task upload`, the panel comes up and the `[E]` lines appear on the serial
  console. That is the only check that an archived BLE stack actually runs; a wrong archive links
  silently, and this is where it would show.
